    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>One step back</strong>: when we report what someone said, the verb usually goes one tense back. The pronouns change too. <em>“I'm tired.” → Amira said she <strong>was</strong> tired. “I work here.” → Tom said he <strong>worked</strong> here. “I've finished.” → Kwame said he <strong>had finished</strong>.</em> More: <em>will → would, can → could, must → had to.</em>",
        "tag": "One step back"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>say</strong> has no person after it: <em>She said (that) she was tired.</em> <strong>tell</strong> always has a person: <em>She told me (that) she was tired.</em> The word <em>that</em> is optional in both.",
        "tag": "say or tell?"
      },
      {
        "html": "If the time or the place is different now, the words change. <em>today → that day, tomorrow → the next day, yesterday → the day before, now → then, here → there, this → that.</em> <em>“I'll call you tomorrow.” → Priya said she would call me <strong>the next day</strong>.</em>",
        "tag": "Time and place words"
      },
      {
        "html": "A reported <strong>question</strong> has normal statement order and no question mark. There is no <em>do / does / did</em>. For a wh-question, keep the question word: <em>“Where do you live?” → She asked me where I <strong>lived</strong>.</em> For a yes / no question, use <strong>if</strong> or <strong>whether</strong>: <em>“Do you like tea?” → He asked if I <strong>liked</strong> tea.</em>",
        "tag": "Reported questions"
      },
      {
        "html": "You do not have to go back if the reporting verb is in the present (<em>She says she's tired.</em>) or if the fact is still true (<em>Tom said he lives in Leeds.</em>). Going back is always safe, so use it when you are not sure.",
        "tag": "When to keep the tense"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>She said me that she was tired.</em> / <em>She told that she was tired.</em> → ✓ <em>She told me that she was tired. She said that she was tired.</em> · ✗ <em>He asked me where did I live.</em> / <em>He asked me do I like tea.</em> → ✓ <em>He asked me where I lived. He asked me if I liked tea.</em> · ✗ <em>Zofia said she is tired and went home.</em> → ✓ <em>Zofia said she was tired and went home.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Reported speech often starts with <em>said, told me</em> or <em>asked</em>. After <em>tell</em> you can also use <em>to</em> + verb for orders and requests (<em>She told me to wait.</em>): see <a href=\"/infinitiv-mit-zu\">Verb patterns: -ing or to</a>. For the word order in reported questions, see <a href=\"/indirekte-fragen\">Indirect questions</a>. The tenses that go back are explained in <a href=\"/praesens\">Present simple</a>, <a href=\"/perfekt\">Past simple</a>, <a href=\"/praeteritum-plusquamperfekt\">Narrative tenses</a> (past perfect), <a href=\"/futur\">Future forms</a> and <a href=\"/modalverben\">Modal verbs</a>.",
